      "question": "Which environmental objectives are eligible for substantial contributions under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "answer": "BIO, Biodiversity and ecosystems, CCA, CCM 1.1, CCM 3.6, CCM 7.1./ CE 3.1, Circular Economy, Climate Change Adaptation, Climate Change Mitigation, PPC, Pollution Prevention and Control, WTR, Water and Marine Resources",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006086:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000007",
      "id": "train:006086:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which environmental objectives are eligible for substantial contributions under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "solution": "BIO, Biodiversity and ecosystems, CCA, CCM 1.1, CCM 3.6, CCM 7.1./ CE 3.1, Circular Economy, Climate Change Adaptation, Climate Change Mitigation, PPC, Pollution Prevention and Control, WTR, Water and Marine Resources",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system designed to provide a common language and framework for understanding which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It aims to help investors make informed decisions and channel investments into projects and activities that contribute substantially to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives.\n\nUnder the EU Taxonomy framework, there are six specific environmental objectives that are eligible for substantial contributions:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that significantly reduce greenhouse gas emissions or enhance carbon sequestration.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that increase resilience to the physical risks of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote water efficiency, reduce pollution, and protect marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support waste reduction, re-use, recycling, and recovery.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that prevent or reduce pollution from various sources.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Activities that contribute to the protection and restoration of biodiversity and natural habitats.\n\nEach of these objectives has detailed criteria and thresholds that must be met to qualify as making a \"substantial contribution\" to sustainability. These criteria ensure that the activities not only contribute positively to the environment but also do no significant harm (DNSH) to any of the other five objectives. This dual assessment ensures a holistic approach to sustainability within the EU Taxonomy framework."
    },
