      "question": "What expenditure components are assessed for compliance with EU Taxonomy criteria?",
      "answer": "additions to leases, additions to property, plant and equipment, agricultural capital expenditure, building renovation measures, day-to-day servicing of assets, internally generated intangible assets, investment property, maintenance and repair, research and development, short-term leases",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006340:qa_pairs_factoid:0006",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000010",
      "id": "train:006340:qa_pairs_factoid:0006",
      "problem": "What expenditure components are assessed for compliance with EU Taxonomy criteria?",
      "solution": "additions to leases, additions to property, plant and equipment, agricultural capital expenditure, building renovation measures, day-to-day servicing of assets, internally generated intangible assets, investment property, maintenance and repair, research and development, short-term leases",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for companies to disclose how environmentally sustainable their economic activities are. It includes six environmental objectives, and for each objective, there are specific criteria that an economic activity must meet to be considered environmentally sustainable.\n\nWhen assessing compliance with the EU Taxonomy criteria, the following expenditure components are typically evaluated:\n\n1. **Qualifying Expenditures**: These are expenditures directly related to the economic activities that aim to achieve one or more of the six environmental objectives. This can include investments in new projects, renovations, upgrades, and other activities that contribute to the sustainability goals outlined in the taxonomy.\n\n2. **Eligible Economic Activities**: The EU Taxonomy defines a list of economic activities that are eligible for assessment under the taxonomy. For these activities to comply, they must meet certain technical screening criteria that ensure they significantly contribute to at least one of the six environmental objectives without causing significant harm to any of the other objectives.\n\n3. **Technical Screening Criteria**: These are detailed criteria provided for each environmental objective. They specify what qualifies as a significant contribution to the objective and what measures need to be taken to avoid significant harm to the other objectives. Compliance with these criteria is essential for an economic activity to be deemed environmentally sustainable according to the EU Taxonomy.\n\n4. **Transitional Activities**: Some economic activities may not fully meet the criteria for being considered environmentally sustainable but are still important for transitioning towards a more sustainable economy. The EU Taxonomy also provides criteria for transitional activities, which are those that make a substantial contribution to one of the environmental objectives and do not cause significant harm to any of the other objectives, while also contributing to the transition to a low-carbon economy.\n\n5. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) Principle**: This principle requires that an economic activity does not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives beyond the one it is contributing to. This means that companies must assess their activities not only on their positive contributions but also on their potential negative impacts on other environmental goals.\n\nIn summary, when assessing compliance with the EU Taxonomy criteria, companies need to evaluate their qualifying expenditures against the eligibility requirements for economic activities, the technical screening criteria, and the DNSH principle. This comprehensive approach ensures that economic activities are genuinely contributing to environmental sustainability without undermining other critical environmental objectives."
    },
