      "problem": "What legal provisions and EU directives are referenced in relation to taxonomy compliance and reporting?",
      "solution": "Article 16 of Regulation (EU) 2020/852, Article 8 of EU Taxonomy Regulation, Directive 2010/31/EU, Disclosures Delegated Act, Regulation (EU) 2017/1369",
      "attempt": "Taxonomy compliance and reporting within the European Union (EU) primarily relate to the Taxonomy Regulation, officially known as Regulation (EU) 2020/852 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 18 June 2020 on the establishment of a framework to facilitate sustainable investment, and amending Regulation (EU) No 2019/2088. This regulation aims to provide companies with a clear understanding of which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable.\n\n### Key Legal Provisions and Directives:\n\n1. **Taxonomy Regulation (2020/852)**:\n   - This regulation establishes a classification system for sustainable economic activities, providing criteria for what qualifies as environmentally sustainable.\n   - It requires certain large companies to disclose information about how their activities align with the taxonomy criteria.\n   - The regulation also mandates that financial market participants and financial advisers must consider the taxonomy when providing advice or making disclosures.\n\n2. **Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) (2019/2088)**:\n   - While not directly a taxonomy regulation, SFDR complements the Taxonomy Regulation by requiring financial market participants and financial advisers to disclose information about the integration of sustainability risks into their investment decision-making processes and the adverse impacts of investment decisions on sustainability factors.\n   - SFDR also requires entities to disclose whether they promote environmental or social characteristics or have a sustainable investment objective, which is closely linked to taxonomy-aligned activities.\n\n3. **Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) (2023/217)**:\n   - The CSRD will replace the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD) and will require large companies to report on a wide range of sustainability issues, including climate change mitigation and adaptation, and the use of the EU Taxonomy.\n   - Companies will need to disclose how their activities contribute to the six environmental objectives outlined in the Taxonomy Regulation.\n\n4. **Disclosure Regulation (2019/2088)**:\n   - This regulation sets out requirements for transparency around the integration of sustainability risks and the consideration of adverse sustainability impacts in the provision of investment advice and the marketing of financial products.\n\nThese regulations collectively aim to enhance transparency and comparability in sustainability-related disclosures, thereby facilitating sustainable investment and helping investors make informed decisions. Compliance with these regulations involves detailed reporting and disclosure obligations, particularly for large companies and financial market participants."
