      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for investors and companies to assess whether an economic activity can be considered environmentally sustainable. For an economic activity to be deemed ecologically sustainable under the EU Taxonomy, it must significantly contribute to at least one of six specific environmental objectives. These objectives are:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that reduce greenhouse gas emissions or enhance carbon sequestration.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that help build resilience against the impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote water efficiency, reduce pollution, and protect marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support waste reduction, re-use, recycling, and recovery of materials and resources.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that reduce the release of harmful substances into the environment.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Activities that contribute to the conservation and restoration of biodiversity and natural habitats.\n\nIn addition to contributing significantly to one or more of these objectives, activities must also do no significant harm (DNSH) to the other environmental objectives and comply with minimum social safeguards as defined by the EU. This comprehensive approach ensures that economic activities not only address environmental challenges but also consider their broader impact on society and the environment."
