      "id": "train:006418:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "problem": "Which regulations and annex are cited as foundational to the EU Taxonomy compliance in the context?",
      "solution": "2023/1214, 2023/2026, 2023/2086, 2023/2485, 2023/3850, 2023/3851, Annex I of Commission Delegated Regulations (EU) 2021/2139, Regulation (EU) 2020/852",
      "attempt": "In the context of EU Taxonomy compliance, the foundational regulation is Regulation (EU) 2020/852 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 18 June 2020 on the establishment of a framework to facilitate sustainable investment, and amending Regulation (EU) 2019/2088 (the \"Taxonomy Regulation\"). This regulation sets out the criteria for determining whether an economic activity is environmentally sustainable.\n\nThe Taxonomy Regulation includes a series of delegated acts and implementing acts that further specify the technical screening criteria for each environmental objective. These delegated acts are developed by the European Commission and adopted based on the initial regulation. They provide detailed guidance on how to assess activities against the criteria laid out in the Taxonomy Regulation.\n\nWhile there isn't a specific \"Annex\" mentioned in the Taxonomy Regulation itself, the regulation does refer to the need for detailed technical screening criteria which are published as Delegated Acts. These Delegated Acts can be seen as supplementary documents that build upon the foundational regulation, providing more granular details necessary for compliance with the taxonomy criteria.\n\nFor instance, Delegated Act (EU) 2021/367 establishes the technical screening criteria for climate change mitigation and adaptation, while other Delegated Acts cover other environmental objectives such as sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nTherefore, when discussing EU Taxonomy compliance, it's essential to consider both the overarching Regulation (EU) 2020/852 and the specific Delegated Acts that provide the detailed criteria for assessing economic activities against the taxonomy's sustainability objectives."
    },
