      "solution": "Notes to the Consolidated Financial Statements, section D) Non-current assets and liabilities, sub-section 18. Property, plant and equipment, sub-section 19. Rights of use from lease arrangements and lease obligations, sub-section 21. Intangible assets other than goodwill",
      "attempt": "Details about non-current assets and liabilities are typically provided in the balance sheet of a company's financial statements. The balance sheet provides a snapshot of a company’s financial position at a specific point in time, detailing its assets, liabilities, and shareholders' equity.\n\n- **Non-current assets** (also known as long-term assets) include items such as property, plant, and equipment; investments in other companies; and intangible assets like patents and trademarks. These are usually listed under the \"Assets\" section of the balance sheet, often broken down into further categories for clarity.\n\n- **Non-current liabilities** (also referred to as long-term liabilities) include long-term loans, bonds payable, and pension obligations. These are detailed in the \"Liabilities\" section of the balance sheet, typically separated from current liabilities which are due within one year or less.\n\nAdditionally, more detailed information about these items might be found in the notes to the financial statements, which provide additional context and explanations regarding the accounting policies used to measure and report these assets and liabilities."
