      "solution": "HR costs, OPEX, building renovations, leasing, maintenance and repairs, research and development, training/retraining measures",
      "attempt": "The term \"taxonomy-eligible\" in the context of operational expenses (OPEX) is not a standard or widely recognized classification. However, if we interpret this question within the context of financial reporting and tax purposes, certain operational expenses can be categorized based on specific criteria set by regulatory bodies or accounting standards.\n\nFor instance, in the context of tax deductions, some operational expenses may be eligible for tax relief depending on the jurisdiction and the nature of the expense. These could include:\n\n1. **Depreciation**: The cost of tangible assets over their useful life.\n2. **Employee Benefits**: Including salaries, wages, bonuses, and other benefits.\n3. **Rent and Utilities**: Costs associated with renting office space and utilities like electricity, water, and gas.\n4. **Marketing and Advertising**: Expenses related to promoting products or services.\n5. **Professional Fees**: Payments to accountants, lawyers, consultants, etc.\n6. **Travel and Entertainment**: Business-related travel costs and entertainment expenses.\n7. **Insurance Premiums**: Costs for various types of insurance coverage.\n8. **Supplies and Materials**: Consumables used in the course of business operations.\n\nIf you're referring to a specific industry or regulatory framework, please provide more details so I can give a more precise answer."
