      "question": "What EU legal frameworks and guidance documents are relevant to reporting under the EU Taxonomy?",
      "answer": "Article 8 of the EU Taxonomy Regulation, Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2178, Disclosures Delegated Act, Draft Commission Notice",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006041:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000013",
      "id": "train:006041:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "What EU legal frameworks and guidance documents are relevant to reporting under the EU Taxonomy?",
      "solution": "Article 8 of the EU Taxonomy Regulation, Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2178, Disclosures Delegated Act, Draft Commission Notice",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language across economic activities for investors, companies, and financial market participants. It helps in identifying which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. Reporting under the EU Taxonomy is governed by several key legal frameworks and guidance documents:\n\n1. **Regulation (EU) 2020/852 on the establishment of a framework to facilitate sustainable investment (the Taxonomy Regulation)**: This regulation establishes the criteria for determining whether an economic activity is environmentally sustainable. It sets out the conditions for activities to qualify as contributing substantially to one or more of six environmental objectives.\n\n2. **Delegated Acts**: These are detailed technical screening criteria that specify how each of the six environmental objectives can be achieved through specific economic activities. The Delegated Acts are legally binding and provide precise definitions and thresholds for what qualifies as sustainable under the Taxonomy.\n\n3. **Disclosure Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2019/2088 on sustainability-related disclosures in the financial services sector)**: This regulation requires financial market participants and financial advisers to disclose information about the integration of sustainability risks, adverse sustainability impacts, and the promotion of environmental characteristics in their financial products. It also mandates the disclosure of information related to the EU Taxonomy.\n\n4. **Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD)**: While not yet fully implemented, this directive will require large companies to report on their sustainability performance, including information on how their activities align with the EU Taxonomy. The CSRD builds upon the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD) and aims to provide more detailed and comparable sustainability information.\n\n5. **Guidance Documents from the European Commission**: The European Commission provides various guidance documents to assist companies and other stakeholders in understanding and implementing the Taxonomy. These include practical guides, FAQs, and updates on the application of the Taxonomy.\n\n6. **Technical Expert Group (TEG) Reports**: Although the TEG has been replaced by the Platform on Sustainable Finance, its reports and recommendations remain valuable resources. They offer insights into the development of the Taxonomy and provide guidance on its implementation.\n\nThese frameworks and documents collectively form the basis for reporting under the EU Taxonomy, ensuring that companies and financial institutions can accurately and transparently communicate their alignment with sustainable economic activities."
    },
