      "problem": "Which classifications are used to evaluate transactions under the EU Taxonomy framework based on eligibility and alignment criteria?",
      "solution": "Taxonomy Regulation, Taxonomy alignment, Taxonomy eligibility, Taxonomy-aligned, Taxonomy-eligible, Taxonomy-non-aligned, Taxonomy-non-eligible",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is a classification system that helps companies and financial market participants identify which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It uses specific criteria to evaluate whether an economic activity can be classified as contributing substantially to one or more of six environmental objectives. These objectives are:\n\n1. Climate change mitigation\n2. Climate change adaptation\n3. Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n4. Transition to a circular economy\n5. Pollution prevention and control\n6. Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\nFor an activity to be classified under the EU Taxonomy, it must meet two main criteria:\n\n1. **Substantial Contribution**: The activity must contribute substantially to at least one of the six environmental objectives listed above.\n   \n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH)**: The activity must not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives.\n\nIn addition to these criteria, there are also performance standards that need to be met, which vary depending on the specific activity and environmental objective. These standards ensure that the activity is aligned with the overarching goal of transitioning to a sustainable economy in line with the Paris Agreement's goals.\n\nCompanies and financial market participants use this framework to disclose information about their sustainability-related investments and activities, helping investors make informed decisions and supporting the transition towards a more sustainable economy."
