      "id": "test:000779:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "problem": "What are the environmental objectives established under the EU Taxonomy Regulation to assess economic sustainability?",
      "solution": "Climate change adaptation, Climate change mitigation, Pollution prevention and control, Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems, Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, Transition to a circular economy",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy Regulation establishes a classification system for environmentally sustainable activities to help investors and companies understand which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. The regulation sets out six environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to in a substantial manner to be considered environmentally sustainable. These objectives are:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that significantly reduce greenhouse gas emissions or enhance carbon sinks.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that increase resilience to climate change impacts.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote water efficiency, reduce pollution, and protect marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support waste reduction, re-use, recycling, and recovery of materials.\n5. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Activities that prevent biodiversity loss and restore degraded ecosystems.\n6. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that reduce the release of harmful substances into the environment.\n\nThese objectives serve as criteria for assessing whether an economic activity can be deemed environmentally sustainable according to the EU Taxonomy Regulation. Companies and financial market participants use these criteria to align their investments and operations with the goals of the European Green Deal, aiming to achieve a net-zero greenhouse gas emission economy by 2050."
