      "problem": "What are the six environmental objectives defined by the EU Taxonomy to classify sustainable economic activities?",
      "solution": "Biodiversity (BIO), Circular economy (CE), Climate change adaptation (CCA), Climate change mitigation (CCM), Pollution (PPC), Water (WTR)",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system designed to provide a common language and framework for investors and companies to assess whether an economic activity can be considered environmentally sustainable. It defines six key environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to in a substantial manner to be classified as sustainable under the taxonomy:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: This objective focuses on reducing greenhouse gas emissions and enhancing carbon sequestration.\n\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: This involves activities that help build resilience against the impacts of climate change, such as extreme weather events.\n\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: This objective aims at ensuring water quality and availability, as well as protecting marine ecosystems.\n\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: This includes activities that promote waste reduction, recycling, and resource efficiency, contributing to a more circular economy.\n\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: This objective targets the reduction of pollution from various sources, including air, soil, and water pollution.\n\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: This involves actions aimed at preserving biodiversity and restoring damaged ecosystems.\n\nEach of these objectives has specific criteria and thresholds that economic activities must meet to be considered as making a substantial contribution to sustainability. The EU Taxonomy also requires that these activities do no significant harm (DNSH) to the other environmental objectives and comply with minimum social safeguards."
