      "question": "Which activities are considered under maintenance and repair expenses as defined by the EU Taxonomy OpEx KPI?",
      "answer": "building refurbishment, day-to-day maintenance of property, plant and equipment, maintenance and repair, maintenance of administrative buildings, systems technology, technical infrastructure",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:005874:qa_pairs_factoid:0009",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000014",
      "id": "train:005874:qa_pairs_factoid:0009",
      "problem": "Which activities are considered under maintenance and repair expenses as defined by the EU Taxonomy OpEx KPI?",
      "solution": "building refurbishment, day-to-day maintenance of property, plant and equipment, maintenance and repair, maintenance of administrative buildings, systems technology, technical infrastructure",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities includes criteria for determining whether an economic activity can be considered environmentally sustainable. Within this framework, the Operational Expenditure (OpEx) Key Performance Indicator (KPI) is used to assess the sustainability of ongoing operational costs associated with these activities. Maintenance and repair expenses fall under this category and are crucial for ensuring that assets continue to function efficiently and sustainably over time.\n\nMaintenance and repair expenses as defined by the EU Taxonomy OpEx KPI typically include:\n\n1. **Routine Maintenance**: This involves regular upkeep and servicing of equipment or infrastructure to prevent breakdowns and ensure optimal performance. Examples include cleaning, lubrication, inspection, and minor adjustments.\n\n2. **Corrective Maintenance**: This refers to repairs made after a failure has occurred. It includes replacing worn-out parts, fixing malfunctions, and restoring equipment to its original condition.\n\n3. **Preventive Maintenance**: Activities aimed at preventing failures before they occur. This might involve scheduled inspections, testing, and replacement of components based on their expected lifespan.\n\n4. **Upgrades and Modernizations**: While not strictly maintenance, certain upgrades and modernizations that improve energy efficiency or reduce environmental impact may also be included if they are necessary to maintain the asset's functionality and sustainability.\n\n5. **Environmental Compliance Costs**: Expenses related to maintaining compliance with environmental regulations, such as waste management, emissions control, and pollution prevention measures.\n\n6. **Training and Support Services**: Costs associated with training staff to perform maintenance tasks effectively and support services that help in managing maintenance operations.\n\nIt's important to note that these expenses must align with the EU Taxonomy's criteria for being considered sustainable. Specifically, they should contribute to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the taxonomy: climate change mitigation, adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nFor detailed guidance and specific criteria, it is advisable to refer to the official documentation provided by the European Commission regarding the EU Taxonomy."
    },
    {
      "category": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_type": "factoid",
      "selection_rank_in_group": 82,
      "split": "train",
      "source_kind": "text",
      "source_sequence_id": "train:text:005888",
      "metadata": {
        "chunk_number": 5888,
        "page_number": 40,
        "titles": [
          "Fulfilment of minimum protection criteria"
        ],
        "subtitles": [],
        "paragraph": "The minimum protection is part of the Taxonomy alignment criteria and is intended to ensure that companies that carry out ecologically sustainable activities do not violate social norms and comply with certain minimum standards that are based on international frameworks.\nArticle 18 of the Taxonomy Regulation cites the OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, the United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, including the fundamental principles and rights of the eight core labour standards set out in the International Labour Organization's Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights for this purpose.\nIn October 2022, the Platform on Sustainable Finance published a report on recommendations for action for the application of minimum protection. Key topics are therefore human and labour rights, the prevention of corruption, the promotion of fair competition and responsible tax practices.\nThe MLP Group is committed to its responsibility for human rights in its supply chain and in its own business operations and therefore adopted a Group-wide declaration of principles. In order to fulfil the requirements of the German Supply Chain Due Diligence Act (LkSG), which MLP SE has been subject to at Group level since January 1, 2024, a risk management process has been implemented to identify, avoid or minimise the risks of human rights violations and damage to the environment. In addition to this, a complaints procedure enables those affected and those who are aware of potential or actual violations to draw attention to risks and violations. Not all requirements can be documented at this point in time. MLP has developed suitable processes and taken all necessary measures to fully comply with the LkSG, which has been in effect since 2024.",
        "classification": "EU Taxonomy",
        "report_name": "MLP_SE.pdf"
      },
