      "question": "Which frameworks are cited in the EU Taxonomy Regulation to ensure adherence to minimum protection standards?",
      "answer": "International Labour Organization's Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, Universal Declaration of Human Rights, eight core labour standards",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:005888:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000014",
      "id": "train:005888:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "Which frameworks are cited in the EU Taxonomy Regulation to ensure adherence to minimum protection standards?",
      "solution": "International Labour Organization's Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work, OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, Universal Declaration of Human Rights, eight core labour standards",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852) does not directly cite specific frameworks for ensuring adherence to minimum social and environmental protection standards. However, it does establish criteria for determining which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. These criteria include significant contributions to one or more of six environmental objectives and compliance with minimum safeguards.\n\nThe regulation refers to the need for activities to comply with certain international standards and guidelines related to labor rights, human rights, and environmental protection. While it doesn't explicitly name frameworks, it aligns closely with several widely recognized frameworks and principles:\n\n1. **International Labour Organization (ILO) Standards**: The ILO's core conventions on labor rights are often referenced as a benchmark for fair labor practices.\n   \n2. **United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (UNGPs)**: These principles provide a framework for companies to respect human rights and address adverse impacts.\n\n3. **OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises**: These guidelines offer recommendations to multinational enterprises on responsible business conduct.\n\n4. **Global Reporting Initiative (GRI) Standards**: Although not mandatory, these standards are often used for sustainability reporting and align well with the transparency requirements of the taxonomy.\n\n5. **ISO 26000 Guidance Standard on Social Responsibility**: This standard provides guidance on social responsibility and can help organizations understand their responsibilities and how to operate in a socially responsible manner.\n\nWhile these frameworks are not directly cited in the regulation, they serve as important references for companies looking to align their activities with the EU Taxonomy's criteria for sustainability. Companies are expected to demonstrate compliance with these broader principles and standards to meet the minimum safeguards required under the regulation."
    },
