      "problem": "Which metrics are used to evaluate taxonomy alignment in the context of EU Taxonomy compliance?",
      "solution": "taxonomy-aligned activities, taxonomy-aligned proportion of sales to total sales, taxonomy-aligned proportions of capital expenditures, taxonomy-aligned proportions of sales, taxonomy-aligned share of CapEx, taxonomy-aligned share of OpEx",
      "attempt": "In the context of evaluating taxonomy alignment for EU Taxonomy compliance, several key metrics and criteria are considered. The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide a common language for sustainable activities, helping investors and companies to identify which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. Here are some of the primary metrics and considerations:\n\n1. **Environmental Objectives**: The EU Taxonomy sets out six environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to significantly. These include climate change mitigation, adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) Principle**: Activities must not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives if they are to be considered compliant with the taxonomy for their primary objective.\n\n3. **Technical Screening Criteria (TSC)**: These are detailed criteria that specify what qualifies as an environmentally sustainable activity under each of the six objectives. They include thresholds for greenhouse gas emissions, energy efficiency, resource use, and other environmental impacts.\n\n4. **Performance Thresholds**: For each objective, there are specific performance thresholds that activities must meet. These thresholds are designed to ensure that only those activities that make a substantial contribution to the relevant environmental objective are classified as sustainable.\n\n5. **Substantial Contribution**: This criterion assesses whether an activity contributes substantially to one or more of the six environmental objectives. It involves a qualitative assessment based on the TSC.\n\n6. **Minimum Safeguards**: In addition to contributing substantially to an environmental objective, activities must also comply with minimum safeguards related to social and human rights standards.\n\n7. **Transparency and Reporting**: Companies need to report on how their activities align with the EU Taxonomy, including details on how they meet the TSC and DNSH requirements. This transparency helps stakeholders understand the sustainability credentials of the company's operations.\n\n8. **Impact Assessment**: An impact assessment may be required to demonstrate that an activity meets the necessary criteria, especially when dealing with complex or innovative projects.\n\nThese metrics and criteria collectively help in assessing whether an economic activity can be considered environmentally sustainable according to the EU Taxonomy. Compliance with these standards is crucial for companies looking to attract sustainable finance and for investors seeking to align their portfolios with green initiatives."
    },
