      "attempt": "CA Immo, as a leading real estate company in Central and Eastern Europe, has taken several steps to address water-related climate risks at its properties. While I don't have specific, up-to-date details on all the measures they've implemented, generally speaking, companies like CA Immo often adopt strategies that include:\n\n1. **Water Efficiency Measures**: Implementing water-saving technologies and practices in buildings to reduce overall water consumption. This can include low-flow fixtures, efficient irrigation systems, and greywater recycling.\n\n2. **Stormwater Management**: Developing strategies to manage stormwater runoff effectively, which helps mitigate flood risks and reduces strain on local drainage systems. This might involve the use of green roofs, permeable pavements, and rain gardens.\n\n3. **Climate-Resilient Design**: Incorporating design elements that enhance resilience against extreme weather events such as heavy rainfall or droughts. This could include better insulation, improved drainage systems, and materials that withstand harsher conditions.\n\n4. **Monitoring and Reporting**: Regularly monitoring water usage and quality, and reporting on these metrics to stakeholders. This helps in identifying areas for improvement and tracking progress over time.\n\n5. **Collaboration with Stakeholders**: Working closely with local authorities, environmental groups, and other stakeholders to share best practices and coordinate efforts in managing water resources sustainably.\n\n6. **Adaptation Planning**: Developing adaptation plans that consider future climate scenarios and potential impacts on water resources. This involves assessing vulnerabilities and implementing proactive measures to protect assets and communities.\n\n7. **Education and Awareness**: Raising awareness among tenants and employees about the importance of water conservation and sustainable practices.\n\nFor the most accurate and detailed information regarding CA Immo's 