      "question": "Which environmental objectives and safeguards were evaluated to ensure alignment with EU Taxonomy standards?",
      "answer": "DNSH - do no significant harm, avoidance of significant adverse effects, compliance with minimum safeguards, significant contribution to an environmental goal, six environmental goals",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "test:000740:qa_pairs_factoid:0011",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000015",
      "id": "test:000740:qa_pairs_factoid:0011",
      "problem": "Which environmental objectives and safeguards were evaluated to ensure alignment with EU Taxonomy standards?",
      "solution": "DNSH - do no significant harm, avoidance of significant adverse effects, compliance with minimum safeguards, significant contribution to an environmental goal, six environmental goals",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is designed to provide a common language and framework for investors and companies to assess the sustainability of economic activities. It includes six environmental objectives that must be met for an activity to be considered environmentally sustainable:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Actions that help build resilience against the impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Measures aimed at conserving water resources and protecting marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Initiatives that promote waste reduction, re-use, recycling, and other circular economy principles.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Efforts to reduce pollution from various sources.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Actions that protect and restore natural habitats and biodiversity.\n\nTo ensure alignment with these objectives, companies and financial institutions evaluate their activities against specific criteria and technical screening criteria (TSC) provided in the EU Taxonomy Regulation. These TSC include:\n\n- **Substantial Contribution**: The activity must make a substantial contribution to one or more of the six environmental objectives without significantly harming any of the others.\n- **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH)**: The activity must not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives if it contributes to one.\n- **Minimum Safeguards**: Compliance with relevant social and labor rights, as well as good governance practices.\n\nThese evaluations involve detailed assessments of the environmental impact of each activity, ensuring that they meet the stringent requirements set out by the EU Taxonomy. This process helps in identifying truly sustainable investments and supports the transition towards a greener economy."
    },
