      "question": "What environmental objectives and criteria are defined under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "answer": "EU Environmental Taxonomy, EU Taxonomy Regulation, four non-climate-related environmental objectives, technical screening criteria, two climate-related environmental objectives",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "test:000777:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000015",
      "id": "test:000777:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "What environmental objectives and criteria are defined under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "solution": "EU Environmental Taxonomy, EU Taxonomy Regulation, four non-climate-related environmental objectives, technical screening criteria, two climate-related environmental objectives",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system designed to provide a common language for investors and companies to understand which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It aims to support the transition to a low-carbon, resource-efficient, and climate-resilient economy in line with the European Green Deal. The framework defines six specific environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to in order to be considered environmentally sustainable:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that significantly contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that help build resilience against the impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote the efficient use of water resources and protect marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support the shift towards a circular economy, including waste prevention, re-use, recycling, and other forms of recovery.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that reduce pollution and its negative effects on human health and the environment.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Activities that contribute to the protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nFor an activity to be classified as environmentally sustainable under the EU Taxonomy, it must:\n- Make a substantial contribution to one or more of these environmental objectives.\n- Do no significant harm (DNSH) to any of the other environmental objectives.\n- Be carried out in compliance with minimum social and governance safeguards.\n\nThese criteria ensure that investments not only contribute positively to the environment but also do not undermine progress in other areas of sustainability. The EU Taxonomy is part of a broader set of measures aimed at mobilizing private capital towards sustainable development goals."
    },
    {
      "category": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_type": "factoid",
