      "problem": "Which articles in the EU Taxonomy Regulation outline environmental objectives, safeguards, and reporting obligations?",
      "solution": "Art. 10-16 EU Taxonomy, Art. 17 of the EU Taxonomy Regulation, Art. 18 of the EU Taxonomy Regulation, Article 8 of the EU Taxonomy Regulation",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852) is structured to provide a framework for sustainable economic activities. The regulation outlines environmental objectives, safeguards, and reporting obligations across several articles. Here's a breakdown of which articles cover these aspects:\n\n1. **Environmental Objectives**: These are outlined primarily in Article 3 of the regulation. It defines the six environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to in order to be considered environmentally sustainable:\n   - Climate change mitigation\n   - Climate change adaptation\n   - Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n   - Transition to a circular economy\n   - Pollution prevention and control\n   - Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\n2. **Safeguards**: Safeguard criteria are detailed in Article 4. These criteria ensure that an activity does not significantly harm any of the six environmental objectives. This is crucial for ensuring that activities classified as sustainable do not inadvertently cause harm to the environment.\n\n3. **Reporting Obligations**: Reporting requirements are covered in Articles 8 and 9. Article 8 requires companies to disclose information on how and to what extent their activities are aligned with the taxonomy. Article 9 mandates that financial market participants and large undertakings report on how they consider the taxonomy in their operations and investments.\n\nThese articles form the core of the regulatory framework designed to promote sustainable investment and economic activities within the European Union."
    },
