      "id": "train:005866:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which financial reporting standards are applied to determine taxonomy-related sales indicators?",
      "solution": "IAS 1.82(a), IFRS, International Financial Reporting Standards, annual financial statements, consolidated income statement",
      "attempt": "Taxonomy-related sales indicators can be determined using various financial reporting standards, depending on the jurisdiction and the specific industry of the company in question. However, two of the most widely recognized sets of standards are:\n\n1. **International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS)**: These are a set of accounting standards developed by the International Accounting Standards Board (IASB) for use by public companies. IFRS provides guidelines on how to report financial information, including sales, which can be relevant when determining taxonomy-related sales indicators. For instance, IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers outlines principles for recognizing revenue, which could be applied to categorize and report sales based on different taxonomies.\n\n2. **Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP)**: In the United States, GAAP is the standard framework of guidelines for financial accounting. The Financial Accounting Standards Board (FASB) develops these standards. Similar to IFRS 15, FASB's ASC 606 (Revenue from Contracts with Customers) provides guidance on revenue recognition that can be used to classify and report sales according to different taxonomies.\n\nWhen determining taxonomy-related sales indicators, companies might also consider industry-specific standards or guidelines provided by professional bodies or regulatory agencies. Additionally, companies may develop their own internal classifications to better align with their business needs and strategic objectives, as long as they comply with the broader financial reporting standards applicable to them.\n\nIt's important to note that the application of these standards requires careful consideration of the specific context and requirements of each standard, as well as any additional disclosures required by regulatory bodies. Consulting with a financial advisor or accountant familiar with these standards is often advisable."
