      "solution": "Computer programming, consultancy and related activities, data processing facilities, on-site management and operation of clients' computer systems, planning and designing computer systems, writing, modifying, testing and supporting software",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities does not directly categorize activities under \"Computer programming, consultancy and related activities\" as a primary focus area. The taxonomy is designed to classify economic activities that make a substantial contribution to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives such as sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nHowever, certain activities within the broader category of \"Computer programming, consultancy and related activities\" (NACE code 62) can contribute to these environmental objectives and thus may be indirectly relevant to the EU Taxonomy. For example:\n\n1. **Development of Software Solutions**: This could include software that supports energy efficiency, renewable energy systems, or waste management.\n   \n2. **Consultancy Services**: These might involve advising companies on how to reduce their carbon footprint, improve resource efficiency, or comply with environmental regulations.\n\n3. **IT Infrastructure Management**: Ensuring that IT infrastructure is energy-efficient and contributes to reducing overall emissions.\n\n4. **Cybersecurity Services**: While not directly related to environmental sustainability, robust cybersecurity can protect critical environmental data and systems.\n\n5. **Data Analysis and Reporting**: Providing tools and services that help organizations monitor and report on their environmental impact.\n\nFor an activity to be considered under the EU Taxonomy, it must meet specific criteria outlined in the regulation, including making a substantial contribution to one or more of the environmental objectives, doing no significant harm to any of the other objectives, and complying with minimum social and governance safeguards.\n\nIf you're looking to align your business activities with the EU Taxonomy, it would be beneficial to consult the detailed criteria provided in the Delegated Act supplementing Regulation (EU) 2020/852 on the establishment of a framework to facilitate sustainable investment. This document provides a comprehensive list of economic activities that qualify as environmentally sustainable according to the taxonomy."
