      "question": "What are the mandatory requirements for economic activities to achieve taxonomy alignment under the EU framework?",
      "answer": "do-no-significant-harm (DNSH) criteria, taxonomy-aligned economic activities, taxonomy-eligible economic activities, technical screening criteria",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006192:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000015",
      "id": "train:006192:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "problem": "What are the mandatory requirements for economic activities to achieve taxonomy alignment under the EU framework?",
      "solution": "do-no-significant-harm (DNSH) criteria, taxonomy-aligned economic activities, taxonomy-eligible economic activities, technical screening criteria",
      "attempt": "The European Union (EU) has developed a Taxonomy Regulation as part of its broader strategy to support the transition to a sustainable economy. This regulation aims to provide a common language and classification system for environmentally sustainable economic activities. For economic activities to achieve taxonomy alignment under the EU framework, they must meet several criteria:\n\n1. **Substantial Contribution**: The activity must make a substantial contribution to at least one of the six environmental objectives outlined in the Taxonomy Regulation:\n   - Climate change mitigation\n   - Climate change adaptation\n   - Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n   - Transition to a circular economy\n   - Pollution prevention and control\n   - Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH)**: Activities must not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives. This means that while an activity may contribute substantially to one objective, it should not undermine the progress towards achieving the other objectives.\n\n3. **Compliance with Technical Screening Criteria**: Economic activities must comply with specific technical screening criteria established by the EU Commission for each of the six environmental objectives. These criteria provide detailed guidance on what qualifies as a substantial contribution and how to ensure DNSH.\n\n4. **Use of Reliable Data and Information**: Companies must base their claims about taxonomy alignment on reliable data and information. This includes using appropriate methodologies and metrics to assess the environmental impact of their activities.\n\n5. **Transparency and Reporting**: Companies are required to disclose information about their taxonomy-aligned activities in their non-financial statements or sustainability reports. This disclosure should include details on how the activities meet the taxonomy criteria, including any relevant data and calculations.\n\n6. **Regular Review and Update**: Given the evolving nature of environmental science and policy, companies need to regularly review and update their taxonomy alignment assessments to reflect new developments and changes in the regulatory landscape.\n\nMeeting these requirements helps ensure that economic activities genuinely contribute to the EU's sustainability goals without causing unintended negative impacts on the environment."
    },
