      "question": "What factors are assessed to determine if an economic activity meets EU Taxonomy eligibility requirements?",
      "answer": "environmental objectives, minimum safeguards, principal adverse impacts, substantial contribution, technical screening criteria",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006292:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000015",
      "id": "train:006292:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "What factors are assessed to determine if an economic activity meets EU Taxonomy eligibility requirements?",
      "solution": "environmental objectives, minimum safeguards, principal adverse impacts, substantial contribution, technical screening criteria",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for companies and financial market participants to disclose information about the environmental sustainability of their economic activities. To be eligible under the EU Taxonomy, an economic activity must meet six specific environmental objectives, and it must also comply with four \"do no significant harm\" (DNSH) criteria. Here are the key factors assessed:\n\n1. **Contribution to Environmental Objectives**: An economic activity must make a substantial contribution to at least one of the following six environmental objectives:\n   - Climate change mitigation\n   - Climate change adaptation\n   - Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n   - Transition to a circular economy\n   - Pollution prevention and control\n   - Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH)**: The activity must not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives. This means that while the activity may contribute positively to one objective, it should not undermine the progress towards achieving the other objectives.\n\n3. **Technical Screening Criteria**: For each environmental objective, there are detailed technical screening criteria that specify what qualifies as a sustainable activity. These criteria include performance thresholds, technical specifications, and other requirements that the activity must meet to be considered sustainable.\n\n4. **Substantiation Requirements**: Companies must substantiate how their activities meet the criteria through documentation and evidence. This includes demonstrating compliance with relevant laws and regulations, as well as providing data on the environmental impact of the activity.\n\n5. **Transparency and Reporting**: Companies are required to report on their activities in line with the EU Taxonomy, including details on how they meet the criteria and any challenges or limitations encountered. This transparency helps stakeholders understand the sustainability credentials of the company's activities.\n\n6. **Alignment with Other Regulations**: Activities must also align with other relevant EU legislation, such as the Energy Efficiency Directive and the Renewable Energy Directive, among others.\n\nBy ensuring that economic activities meet these criteria, the EU Taxonomy aims to promote investments that support the transition to a more sustainable economy while avoiding greenwashing and ensuring that all environmental objectives are respected."
    },
