      "problem": "Which EU regulations and delegated acts are referenced in the context of taxonomy compliance?",
      "solution": "2020/852, 2022/1214, 2023/2486, Commission Delegated Regulation, EU Taxonomy Regulation",
      "attempt": "The context of taxonomy compliance within the European Union primarily refers to the Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852), also known as the EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities. This regulation aims to provide companies with a common language and framework to assess whether their economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable.\n\nIn addition to the main regulation, there are several Delegated Acts that have been adopted to specify the technical screening criteria for different environmental objectives under the Taxonomy Regulation. These Delegated Acts are:\n\n1. **Delegated Act (EU) 2021/36** - This act sets out the technical screening criteria for climate change mitigation.\n2. **Delegated Act (EU) 2021/2178** - This act establishes the technical screening criteria for climate change adaptation.\n3. **Delegated Act (EU) 2022/1294** - This act provides the technical screening criteria for sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources.\n4. **Delegated Act (EU) 2022/1295** - This act sets out the technical screening criteria for transition to a circular economy.\n5. **Delegated Act (EU) 2022/1296** - This act establishes the technical screening criteria for pollution prevention and control.\n\nThese Delegated Acts are crucial because they provide detailed guidance on how to determine if an activity contributes substantially to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the Taxonomy Regulation. Companies must comply with these criteria when reporting on their sustainability efforts and when claiming that their investments or activities are environmentally sustainable according to the EU Taxonomy."
