      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide a common language for sustainable finance. It identifies which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable based on their contribution to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives. In the context of lighting-related products and services, several categories can contribute to the revenues allocated to EU Taxonomy economic activities:\n\n1. **Energy-Efficient Lighting Products**: This includes LED (Light Emitting Diode) lamps and luminaires, which consume significantly less energy than traditional lighting technologies like incandescent or fluorescent lights. These products contribute to climate change mitigation by reducing greenhouse gas emissions.\n\n2. **Smart Lighting Systems**: These systems use advanced technology to optimize lighting usage based on occupancy, daylight availability, and other factors. They can reduce overall energy consumption in buildings and public spaces, thereby contributing to both climate change mitigation and resource efficiency.\n\n3. **Renewable Energy-Based Lighting Solutions**: Products that integrate renewable energy sources such as solar panels into lighting systems can also contribute to sustainability goals. Solar-powered streetlights and indoor lighting solutions are examples of this category.\n\n4. **Services Related to Lighting Efficiency and Management**: This includes consulting services for designing energy-efficient lighting systems, maintenance services that ensure optimal performance of lighting equipment, and software solutions that help manage and monitor lighting usage. Such services support the transition to more sustainable lighting practices.\n\n5. **Recycling and Waste Management Services for Lighting Equipment**: Services that facilitate the recycling of old lighting equipment and the proper disposal of hazardous materials (like mercury in some older lighting technologies) contribute to the circular economy and waste prevention, aligning with the EU Taxonomy's environmental objectives.\n\nThese products and services not only help businesses and consumers save money through reduced energy costs but also play a crucial role in achieving broader environmental goals, making them eligible for revenues allocated under the EU Taxonomy framework."
    },
