      "question": "Which terms are used to classify economic activities under the EU Taxonomy Regulation?",
      "answer": "taxonomy alignment, taxonomy eligibility, taxonomy-aligned economic activities, taxonomy-eligible capital expenditures",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "test:000778:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "test:000778:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "Which terms are used to classify economic activities under the EU Taxonomy Regulation?",
      "solution": "taxonomy alignment, taxonomy eligibility, taxonomy-aligned economic activities, taxonomy-eligible capital expenditures",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy Regulation uses specific terms to classify economic activities based on their environmental sustainability. These terms are designed to help companies and investors identify which activities contribute significantly to achieving climate change mitigation and adaptation goals, as well as other environmental objectives. The key terms include:\n\n1. **Environmental Objectives**: These are the overarching goals that economic activities must contribute to in order to be considered environmentally sustainable. The regulation currently focuses on six environmental objectives:\n   - Climate change mitigation\n   - Climate change adaptation\n   - Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n   - Transition to a circular economy\n   - Pollution prevention and control\n   - Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\n2. **Substantially Contributing Economic Activities**: An activity is considered to substantially contribute if it directly contributes to one or more of the environmental objectives listed above.\n\n3. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) Principle**: This principle ensures that an activity does not cause significant harm to any of the other environmental objectives. It's important because an activity can contribute to one objective but still cause significant harm to another.\n\n4. **Minimum Safeguards**: These are social and governance criteria that must be met for an activity to be considered sustainable. They ensure that the activity respects human rights and labor standards.\n\n5. **Technical Screening Criteria (TSC)**: These are detailed criteria developed by the European Commission to determine whether an economic activity qualifies as environmentally sustainable. TSCs provide specific thresholds and requirements for each of the environmental objectives.\n\nThese terms and concepts form the basis of the EU Taxonomy Regulation, guiding businesses and financial markets towards investments that support the transition to a more sustainable economy."
