      "question": "What key performance indicators are used to assess compliance with the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "answer": "EU Taxonomy KPIs, taxonomy alignment, taxonomy eligibility, taxonomy-eligible economic activities",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "test:000791:qa_pairs_factoid:0015",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "test:000791:qa_pairs_factoid:0015",
      "problem": "What key performance indicators are used to assess compliance with the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "solution": "EU Taxonomy KPIs, taxonomy alignment, taxonomy eligibility, taxonomy-eligible economic activities",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for companies to disclose how their economic activities align with the goals of sustainable finance as defined by the European Union. It focuses on six environmental objectives: climate change mitigation, climate change adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems.\n\nTo assess compliance with the EU Taxonomy framework, several key performance indicators (KPIs) are used. These KPIs help companies demonstrate that their activities contribute substantially to one or more of the six environmental objectives without causing significant harm to the other objectives. Here are some of the key KPIs:\n\n1. **Substantial Contribution**: This involves demonstrating that an economic activity contributes significantly to at least one of the six environmental objectives. The contribution must be substantial, meaning it must make a meaningful impact towards achieving the objective.\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH)**: Companies must ensure that their activities do not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives. This requires careful assessment and management of potential negative impacts.\n\n3. **Technical Screening Criteria (TSC)**: These are detailed criteria provided in the EU Taxonomy Regulation that specify what qualifies as a \"substantial contribution\" to each of the six environmental objectives. Compliance with these criteria is essential for qualifying under the taxonomy.\n\n4. **Environmental Thresholds**: For certain activities, specific thresholds are set to define what constitutes a substantial contribution. For example, in the context of renewable energy, there might be specific requirements regarding the type of technology used and its efficiency.\n\n5. **Sustainability-Linked Financial Products**: For financial products linked to the taxonomy, additional KPIs may include the proportion of the portfolio that meets taxonomy criteria, changes in this proportion over time, and alignment with the taxonomy's technical screening criteria.\n\n6. **Transparency and Reporting**: Companies are required to report annually on their taxonomy-aligned activities, including the methodologies used to determine alignment, the data sources, and any assumptions made. Transparency is crucial for building trust and ensuring accountability.\n\n7. **Impact Assessment**: Companies need to conduct impact assessments to evaluate the environmental benefits and potential harms of their activities. This includes both quantitative and qualitative assessments.\n\n8. **Stakeholder Engagement**: Engaging with stakeholders, including investors, customers, and regulators, can provide valuable insights into the effectiveness of sustainability measures and help identify areas for improvement.\n\nThese KPIs are designed to ensure that economic activities genuinely support the transition to a sustainable economy while avoiding unintended negative consequences. Compliance with the EU Taxonomy is becoming increasingly important for companies looking to attract sustainable investment and meet regulatory requirements."
    },
