      "question": "Which international frameworks guide Hapag-Lloyd's compliance with global tax regulations and EU Taxonomy governance standards?",
      "answer": "Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) programme, global minimum level of taxation (Pillar II), legislation on a global minimum level of taxation, redistribution of taxation rights (Pillar I)",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "test:000797:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "test:000797:qa_pairs_factoid:0003",
      "problem": "Which international frameworks guide Hapag-Lloyd's compliance with global tax regulations and EU Taxonomy governance standards?",
      "solution": "Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) programme, global minimum level of taxation (Pillar II), legislation on a global minimum level of taxation, redistribution of taxation rights (Pillar I)",
      "attempt": "Hapag-Lloyd, as a major container shipping company, would be guided by several international frameworks to ensure compliance with global tax regulations and EU Taxonomy governance standards. However, it's important to note that the EU Taxonomy specifically relates to sustainable activities and is more directly applicable to environmental sustainability reporting rather than general tax compliance. Here’s an overview of relevant frameworks:\n\n1. **OECD Transfer Pricing Guidelines**: These guidelines provide a framework for multinational enterprises (MNEs) and tax administrations on how to apply the arm's length principle to transfer pricing in relation to cross-border related party transactions. Hapag-Lloyd, being a multinational corporation, must adhere to these guidelines to ensure its intercompany transactions are priced at market value.\n\n2. **EU Directives and Regulations**: As a European company, Hapag-Lloyd must comply with various EU directives and regulations, including those concerning corporate taxation. This includes the Anti-Tax Avoidance Directive (ATAD), which aims to prevent aggressive tax planning practices within the EU.\n\n3. **EU Taxonomy Regulation**: The EU Taxonomy Regulation provides a classification system for environmentally sustainable economic activities. While this regulation is primarily aimed at financial disclosures and investment activities, companies like Hapag-Lloyd may need to report on their alignment with the taxonomy if they engage in activities that fall under its scope or if they are subject to disclosure requirements under the Non-Financial Reporting Directive (NFRD) or the Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD).\n\n4. **General International Tax Compliance**: Hapag-Lloyd also needs to comply with broader international tax compliance standards such as the Common Reporting Standard (CRS) and the Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act (FATCA). These standards require the reporting of financial accounts held by non-resident entities to tax authorities.\n\n5. **Country-by-Country Reporting (CbCR)**: Under the OECD Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) Action Plan, MNEs are required to submit CbCR reports detailing their global operations and profits by country. This helps tax authorities understand the global footprint of MNEs and ensures fair tax contributions in each jurisdiction.\n\n6. **Local Tax Laws and Regulations**: In addition to international frameworks, Hapag-Lloyd must comply with local tax laws and regulations in all countries where it operates.\n\nBy adhering to these frameworks, Hapag-Lloyd can ensure that its tax practices are transparent, compliant, and aligned with both global and regional regulatory expectations."
    },
    {
      "category": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_type": "factoid",
