      "question": "What classifications of economic activities are defined in the EU Taxonomy for alignment purposes?",
      "answer": "Taxonomy-aligned, Taxonomy-eligible, Taxonomy-non-aligned, Taxonomy-non-eligible",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "test:000799:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "test:000799:qa_pairs_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "What classifications of economic activities are defined in the EU Taxonomy for alignment purposes?",
      "solution": "Taxonomy-aligned, Taxonomy-eligible, Taxonomy-non-aligned, Taxonomy-non-eligible",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system designed to provide a common language and framework for companies and financial market participants to disclose information about their environmentally sustainable economic activities. It aims to help investors identify which investments are truly contributing to environmental sustainability goals as defined by the European Union.\n\nThe taxonomy defines six environmental objectives that economic activities must contribute to in order to be considered environmentally sustainable:\n\n1. **Climate Change Mitigation**: Activities that reduce greenhouse gas emissions.\n2. **Climate Change Adaptation**: Activities that help build resilience against the impacts of climate change.\n3. **Sustainable Use and Protection of Water and Marine Resources**: Activities that promote the efficient use of water resources and protect marine ecosystems.\n4. **Transition to a Circular Economy**: Activities that support the transition towards a circular economy, reducing waste and promoting recycling and reuse.\n5. **Pollution Prevention and Control**: Activities that prevent or reduce pollution.\n6. **Protection and Restoration of Biodiversity and Ecosystems**: Activities that contribute to the protection and restoration of biodiversity and natural habitats.\n\nFor an activity to be classified under the EU Taxonomy, it must:\n- Make a substantial contribution to one or more of these environmental objectives.\n- Do no significant harm (DNSH) to any of the other environmental objectives.\n- Comply with minimum social safeguards as defined by the International Labour Organization (ILO).\n\nThis framework helps ensure that economic activities are not only environmentally beneficial but also socially responsible, aligning with broader sustainability goals."
