      "question": "What are the conditions under the EU Taxonomy for an economic activity to qualify as environmentally sustainable?",
      "answer": "do no significant harm, environmentally sustainable, no significant harm to any of the other environmental objectives, substantial contribution to at least one of the environmental objectives",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006081:qa_pairs_factoid:0016",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "train:006081:qa_pairs_factoid:0016",
      "problem": "What are the conditions under the EU Taxonomy for an economic activity to qualify as environmentally sustainable?",
      "solution": "do no significant harm, environmentally sustainable, no significant harm to any of the other environmental objectives, substantial contribution to at least one of the environmental objectives",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language and framework for companies and financial market participants to disclose information about their environmental sustainability. For an economic activity to be considered environmentally sustainable under the EU Taxonomy, it must meet six specific criteria:\n\n1. **Substantial Contribution**: The activity must make a substantial contribution to one or more of the six environmental objectives outlined in the taxonomy:\n   - Climate change mitigation\n   - Climate change adaptation\n   - Sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources\n   - Transition to a circular economy\n   - Pollution prevention and control\n   - Protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH)**: The activity must not cause significant harm to any of the other five environmental objectives. This ensures that while promoting one objective, the activity does not undermine others.\n\n3. **Compliance with Minimum Social Safeguards**: The activity must comply with relevant social and governance requirements, such as those related to labor rights, human rights, and anti-corruption measures.\n\n4. **Technical Screening Criteria**: The activity must meet detailed technical screening criteria set out in the Delegated Acts adopted by the European Commission. These criteria specify what qualifies as environmentally sustainable within each of the six objectives.\n\n5. **Environmental Performance Standards**: The activity must achieve certain performance standards that reflect best practices in terms of environmental impact.\n\n6. **Transparency and Disclosure**: Companies must disclose how they assess whether their activities meet the criteria of the taxonomy, including any methodologies used and data sources relied upon.\n\nThese criteria ensure that economic activities classified as sustainable under the EU Taxonomy contribute positively to environmental goals without causing significant harm to other environmental objectives and adhere to high social and governance standards."
    },
