      "problem": "What international frameworks are referenced in the Taxonomy Regulation to ensure compliance with minimum safeguards?",
      "solution": "International Bill of Human Rights, International Labour Organization's core labor standards, OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, United Nations Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights",
      "attempt": "The Taxonomy Regulation, officially known as the EU Taxonomy Climate Delegated Act and part of the broader EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities, references several international frameworks to ensure compliance with minimum safeguards. These frameworks are designed to promote sustainable economic activities while ensuring social and environmental standards are met. Here are some key international frameworks mentioned or implied within the context of the Taxonomy Regulation:\n\n1. **Paris Agreement**: The Taxonomy Regulation aligns with the goals of the Paris Agreement on climate change, which aims to limit global warming to well below 2 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels and pursue efforts to limit it to 1.5 degrees Celsius.\n\n2. **United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)**: While not directly cited, the Taxonomy Regulation supports many of the SDGs, particularly those related to climate action (Goal 13), affordable and clean energy (Goal 7), and responsible consumption and production (Goal 12).\n\n3. **International Labour Organization (ILO) Conventions**: The regulation ensures that activities comply with core labor standards as defined by the ILO, including freedom of association and the effective recognition of the right to collective bargaining, elimination of all forms of forced or compulsory labor, effective abolition of child labor, and elimination of discrimination in respect of employment and occupation.\n\n4. **OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises**: Although not explicitly mentioned, the principles of responsible business conduct outlined in these guidelines are aligned with the Taxonomy's objectives of promoting sustainable activities.\n\n5. **Montreal Protocol**: The regulation also indirectly supports the Montreal Protocol on Substances that Deplete the Ozone Layer, as it promotes activities that do not harm the ozone layer.\n\n6. **Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)**: The Taxonomy Regulation indirectly supports biodiversity conservation through its promotion of sustainable land use and management practices.\n\nThese frameworks collectively ensure that economic activities classified under the EU Taxonomy contribute positively to environmental sustainability while respecting social and governance criteria. Compliance with these frameworks is essential for activities to be considered environmentally sustainable under the Taxonomy Regulation."
