      "question": "Which fiscal years are relevant to the reporting changes and future alignment plans under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "answer": "2021 financial year, 2022 fiscal year, 2023, 2024 reporting period",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_id": "train:006252:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "train:006252:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which fiscal years are relevant to the reporting changes and future alignment plans under the EU Taxonomy framework?",
      "solution": "2021 financial year, 2022 fiscal year, 2023, 2024 reporting period",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy for sustainable activities is a classification system that aims to provide a common language for investors and companies to assess the sustainability of economic activities. It was introduced as part of the European Union's broader strategy to achieve its climate and environmental goals.\n\nRegarding fiscal years relevant to reporting changes and future alignment plans under the EU Taxonomy framework:\n\n1. **2021-2022**: These were early years in which companies started preparing for the implementation of the EU Taxonomy. The first set of delegated acts under the Taxonomy Regulation came into force on January 1, 2022, covering climate change mitigation and adaptation. Companies began to align their reporting with these requirements.\n\n2. **2023**: This year saw the extension of the scope of the EU Taxonomy to cover additional environmental objectives beyond climate change, such as water and land use, pollution, and biodiversity. Companies had to start reporting on these new objectives from this fiscal year onwards.\n\n3. **2024 and Beyond**: As the EU continues to refine and expand the Taxonomy, more detailed reporting requirements will be phased in over time. For instance, the EU has proposed to include nuclear and natural gas activities under the Taxonomy, subject to certain conditions, which would affect reporting starting from 2026 if approved.\n\nCompanies listed on EU-regulated markets with more than 500 employees and more than €150 million in turnover or total assets must disclose how their activities align with the EU Taxonomy. These disclosures are expected to become increasingly detailed and comprehensive as the framework evolves.\n\nIt's important for companies to stay updated with the latest regulatory developments and guidance from the European Commission and other relevant bodies to ensure compliance with the evolving EU Taxonomy requirements."
    },
