      "question_id": "train:006332:qa_pairs_factoid:0005",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000016",
      "id": "train:006332:qa_pairs_factoid:0005",
      "problem": "Which reporting standards are applied to determine taxonomy-aligned sales revenue in the Delegated Act on Reporting Obligations?",
      "solution": "Delegated Act on Reporting Obligations, IAS 1.82 (a), taxonomy-aligned economic activities",
      "attempt": "The Delegated Act on Reporting Obligations, which is part of the Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) under the European Union's regulatory framework, does not directly specify a single set of reporting standards for determining taxonomy-aligned sales revenue. However, it does require financial market participants and financial advisers to disclose information about their activities that contribute to environmentally sustainable economic activities as defined by the EU Taxonomy.\n\nFor the purposes of aligning sales revenue with the EU Taxonomy, entities typically refer to the following:\n\n1. **EU Taxonomy Regulation**: This regulation provides the criteria for determining whether an economic activity is environmentally sustainable. It includes thresholds for significant contributions to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives such as water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and biodiversity protection.\n\n2. **European Financial Reporting Advisory Group (EFRAG)**: EFRAG has developed draft Taxonomy-aligned reporting standards and guidelines that help companies report on their alignment with the EU Taxonomy. These guidelines are designed to provide clarity on how to apply the EU Taxonomy criteria to financial and non-financial disclosures.\n\n3. **International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB)**: Although not specific to the EU Taxonomy, the ISSB's standards may be relevant for broader sustainability reporting practices, which can complement the EU Taxonomy requirements.\n\n4. **Global Reporting Initiative (GRI)**: GRI standards can also be used in conjunction with the EU Taxonomy to provide a comprehensive view of sustainability performance, although they are not specifically aligned with the EU Taxonomy criteria.\n\nIn practice, companies will need to use these frameworks in combination to ensure that their reporting on taxonomy-aligned sales revenue is accurate and compliant with the SFDR and related regulations. They should consult the latest guidance from EFRAG and other relevant bodies to ensure they are using the most up-to-date and appropriate methodologies for their reporting obligations."
    },
    {
      "category": "EU Taxonomy",
      "question_type": "factoid",
      "selection_rank_in_group": 253,
