      "id": "train:006328:qa_pairs_non_factoid:0011",
      "problem": "Why is Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 considered a foundational element of the EU Taxonomy framework, and how does its adaptation by Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2485 reflect the evolving scope of sustainable economic activities?",
      "solution": "Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 serves as a foundational regulation that was later adapted by Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2485 to include new economic activities, reflecting the evolving scope of the taxonomy framework.",
      "attempt": "Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2139 is a foundational element of the EU Taxonomy framework because it sets out detailed criteria for determining which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable under the EU Taxonomy Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2020/852). The EU Taxonomy aims to provide a common language and classification system for sustainable economic activities, helping investors and companies to identify and invest in projects that contribute to environmental sustainability.\n\nThe regulation outlines six environmental objectives: climate change mitigation, climate change adaptation, sustainable use and protection of water and marine resources, transition to a circular economy, pollution prevention and control, and protection and restoration of biodiversity and ecosystems. It provides specific technical screening criteria for each objective, which must be met for an activity to be deemed taxonomy-aligned.\n\nDelegated Regulation (EU) 2023/2485 reflects the evolving scope of sustainable economic activities by updating and adapting these criteria based on new scientific evidence, technological advancements, and societal needs. This adaptation ensures that the EU Taxonomy remains relevant and effective in promoting sustainable finance and economic activities. For instance, it may include more stringent criteria for certain activities to better align with the latest understanding of what constitutes sustainable practices or introduce new criteria to address emerging environmental challenges.\n\nThis continuous refinement process is crucial as it allows the EU Taxonomy to stay aligned with the overarching goal of achieving the European Green Deal's climate neutrality target by 2050 while also addressing other environmental concerns. By doing so, it helps ensure that investments are directed towards activities that genuinely contribute to long-term environmental sustainability, thereby supporting the transition to a greener economy."
    },
