      "id": "test:000755:qa_pairs_non_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "Why do the criteria for climate change mitigation under the EU Taxonomy disqualify data centres (Activity 8.1) based on the global warming potential of refrigerants, despite compliance with other regulations?",
      "solution": "The data centres failed to meet the criteria for substantial contribution to climate change mitigation because the global warming potential (GWP) of refrigerants used in their cooling systems exceeded the defined maximum value of 675, despite complying with Regulation (EU) 517/2014 on fluorinated greenhouse gases.",
      "attempt": "The EU Taxonomy is a classification system designed to provide a common language and framework for investors to understand which economic activities can be considered environmentally sustainable. It sets out specific criteria that economic activities must meet in order to be considered as making a substantial contribution to one or more of six environmental objectives, including climate change mitigation.\n\nFor data centers (Activity 8.1), the EU Taxonomy includes stringent requirements related to greenhouse gas emissions, particularly regarding the use of refrigerants with high global warming potential (GWP). The rationale behind this is rooted in the significant impact that certain refrigerants can have on climate change. Refrigerants with high GWP values contribute significantly to global warming when released into the atmosphere, even in small quantities. This is because they trap heat in the Earth's atmosphere much more effectively than carbon dioxide (CO2).\n\nData centers often use refrigerants in their cooling systems to manage the heat generated by servers and other IT equipment. If these cooling systems use refrigerants with high GWP, such as some types of hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), the overall carbon footprint of the data center can be considerably higher than it would be if lower-GWP alternatives were used.\n\nEven if a data center complies with other regulations, such as those set by the European F-Gas Regulation, which aims to reduce the emissions of fluorinated gases, the EU Taxonomy may still disqualify the activity if it does not meet its own specific criteria. The F-Gas Regulation focuses on reducing the total amount of fluorinated gases placed on the market and managing their emissions throughout their lifecycle, but it does not necessarily align perfectly with the EU Taxonomy’s criteria for climate change mitigation.\n\nIn summary, the EU Taxonomy's criteria for climate change mitigation are designed to ensure that economic activities not only comply with existing regulations but also make a substantial contribution to mitigating climate change. For data centers, this means using refrigerants with low GWP values to minimize their contribution to global warming, even if they are compliant with other regulatory frameworks."
