      "id": "train:005870:qa_pairs_non_factoid:0002",
      "problem": "Why does Delegated Regulation 2021/2178 require training costs to be included in both the numerator and denominator of the OpEx KPI calculation, and how does this align with EU Taxonomy objectives?",
      "solution": "Training costs are included in the numerator of the OpEx KPI calculation in accordance with Delegated Regulation 2021/2178, and these cost centres must also be included in the denominator.",
      "attempt": "Delegated Regulation (EU) 2021/2178, which amends Regulation (EU) 2019/2089 on disclosure of sustainability-related information by financial market participants and financial advisers, aims to enhance transparency and comparability of sustainability-related disclosures. The requirement for training costs to be included in both the numerator and denominator of the Operating Expenditure (OpEx) Key Performance Indicator (KPI) calculation is part of these enhancements.\n\nThe inclusion of training costs in both parts of the OpEx KPI calculation serves several purposes:\n\n1. **Transparency**: By including training costs in both the numerator (total operating expenses) and the denominator (revenue), it ensures that all relevant costs are accounted for, providing a more accurate picture of operational efficiency. This transparency helps stakeholders better understand the cost structure and performance of the organization.\n\n2. **Consistency**: Including training costs in both components of the KPI ensures consistency across different organizations and sectors. This alignment facilitates meaningful comparisons between entities, which is crucial for investors and other stakeholders who rely on such metrics to make informed decisions.\n\n3. **Alignment with EU Taxonomy Objectives**: The EU Taxonomy aims to provide a classification system for environmentally sustainable economic activities. By requiring the inclusion of training costs, the regulation encourages organizations to invest in skills development related to sustainability. Training can include topics such as environmental management, sustainable practices, and compliance with environmental regulations. Such investments are critical for long-term sustainability and align with the broader goals of the EU Taxonomy, which seeks to promote activities that contribute to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives.\n\n4. **Promoting Sustainable Practices**: Training employees on sustainability issues can lead to improved environmental performance and innovation in sustainable business practices. By recognizing these costs in the KPI, the regulation incentivizes organizations to allocate resources towards sustainability education and awareness, thereby fostering a culture of sustainability within the organization.\n\nIn summary, the requirement to include training costs in both the numerator and denominator of the OpEx KPI calculation under Delegated Regulation 2021/2178 enhances transparency and consistency in financial reporting while promoting investment in sustainability-related training. This approach aligns with the overarching objectives of the EU Taxonomy by encouraging organizations to develop the necessary skills and knowledge to operate sustainably and contribute positively to environmental goals."
    },
