      "problem": "Why was a cross-functional team necessary for determining taxonomy-eligible activities at TAKKT, and how did the involvement of Group Sustainability and Group Accounting contribute to this process?",
      "solution": "The cross-functional team at TAKKT was set up to determine the taxonomy-eligible activities. Its core team comprised the Group Sustainability and Group Accounting departments.",
      "attempt": "A cross-functional team was necessary for determining taxonomy-eligible activities at TAKKT because such a task requires input from various departments to ensure that all relevant aspects of the company's operations are considered. The taxonomy in question likely refers to the EU Taxonomy, which is a classification system designed to help companies identify and disclose environmentally sustainable economic activities. This system is complex and requires detailed knowledge of both environmental criteria and financial activities.\n\nThe involvement of Group Sustainability and Group Accounting contributed significantly to this process in the following ways:\n\n1. **Group Sustainability**: This department would have deep expertise in sustainability standards, regulations, and best practices. They could provide insights into which activities align with environmental goals and sustainability criteria as defined by the EU Taxonomy. Their role would be crucial in identifying activities that contribute positively to climate change mitigation and adaptation, as well as other environmental objectives.\n\n2. **Group Accounting**: This department would bring a financial perspective to the table, ensuring that the activities identified as taxonomy-eligible meet not only environmental but also financial reporting standards. They can help in assessing the financial impact of these activities, verifying compliance with accounting principles, and ensuring that the information reported is accurate and reliable. Additionally, they can assist in integrating the taxonomy requirements into the company’s broader financial strategy and reporting framework.\n\nBy combining the expertise of these two departments, TAKKT could ensure that its taxonomy-eligible activities are both environmentally sound and financially robust, thereby meeting the dual objectives of sustainability and transparency required by the EU Taxonomy. This collaborative approach helps in creating a comprehensive and accurate assessment of the company's sustainable activities, facilitating better decision-making and stakeholder communication."
