      "attempt": "The taxonomy referred to here is likely the EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities, which is a classification system designed to help investors identify environmentally sustainable economic activities. For an activity to be considered taxonomy-aligned, it must meet specific criteria that ensure it contributes substantially to one of six environmental objectives and does not significantly harm any of the other objectives. Additionally, the activity must comply with minimum safeguards and do no significant harm (DNSH) to the other environmental objectives.\n\nTaxonomy-eligible but non-aligned activities refer to those that could potentially contribute to the environmental objectives but do not fully meet all the criteria required to be classified as taxonomy-aligned. This could be due to several reasons:\n\n1. **Criteria Not Fully Met**: The activity might not meet the technical screening criteria set out in the delegated acts for each environmental objective. These criteria are quite detailed and include requirements such as greenhouse gas emissions thresholds, energy efficiency standards, or biodiversity protection measures.\n\n2. **Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) Principle**: Even if an activity contributes positively to one environmental objective, it must also ensure that it does not cause significant harm to the other five objectives. If an activity fails this test, it cannot be classified as taxonomy-aligned.\n\n3. **Minimum Safeguards**: Activities must adhere to certain social and governance standards, including labor rights, human rights, and anti-corruption measures. Failure to meet these minimum safeguards can disqualify an activity from being taxonomy-aligned.\n\n4. **Regulatory Updates**: The taxonomy framework is continuously updated, and new criteria or stricter interpretations of existing criteria may have been applied in 2023, leading to some activities previously thought to be eligible now failing to meet the alignment criteria.\n\nIn summary, activities that are taxonomy-eligible but non-aligned in 2023 did not meet all the stringent criteria required to be classified as taxonomy-aligned, either because they failed to meet the specific technical screening criteria, violated the DNSH principle, did not comply with minimum safeguards, or were affected by regulatory updates."
