      "problem": "Which external groups does ATOSS Software AG engage with to ensure sustainable business practices?",
      "solution": "(potential) future employees, analysts, customers, partner network, shareholders and investors, suppliers and service providers, the media",
      "attempt": "ATOSS Software AG, as a company focused on workforce management solutions, likely engages with several external groups to ensure sustainable business practices. While specific details about all their partnerships and collaborations may not be publicly detailed in every instance, companies like ATOSS typically interact with the following types of external groups:\n\n1. **Regulatory Bodies**: Compliance with local and international laws is crucial for sustainability. ATOSS would work closely with regulatory bodies to ensure that its operations and products adhere to legal standards.\n\n2. **Industry Associations**: Participation in industry associations can provide insights into best practices and help shape policies that promote sustainability within the industry. For example, they might be part of associations related to software development or human resources technology.\n\n3. **Environmental Groups**: Engaging with environmental organizations can help ATOSS understand how to reduce its carbon footprint and adopt more eco-friendly practices in its operations and product lifecycle.\n\n4. **Community Organizations**: Collaboration with community groups can help ATOSS contribute positively to the communities where it operates, fostering goodwill and sustainable relationships.\n\n5. **Suppliers and Partners**: Ensuring that suppliers and partners also adhere to sustainable practices is important for maintaining an overall sustainable supply chain. This includes ethical sourcing of materials and services.\n\n6. **Customers and Stakeholders**: Feedback from customers and other stakeholders can provide valuable input on how ATOSS can improve its sustainability efforts. Engaging these groups through surveys, focus groups, and other means can be beneficial.\n\n7. **Academic Institutions and Research Centers**: Partnering with universities and research centers can provide ATOSS with cutting-edge knowledge and innovations that support sustainable business practices.\n\n8. **Non-Governmental Organizations (NGOs)**: NGOs often have expertise in specific areas of sustainability and can offer guidance and certification programs that help businesses meet higher standards of sustainability.\n\nFor the most accurate and detailed information regarding ATOSS Software AG's specific engagements and partnerships, it would be best to refer directly to their official communications, such as annual reports, sustainability reports, or their corporate website."
