      "id": "train:001535:qa_pairs_factoid:0001",
      "problem": "Which gases contribute to the greenhouse effect and are measured for their environmental impact?",
      "solution": "carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>), hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), methane (CH4), nitrogen trifluoride (NF<sub>3</sub>), nitrous oxide (N2O), perfluorocarbons (PFCs), sulphur hexafluoride (SF<sub>e</sub>)",
      "attempt": "The greenhouse effect is caused primarily by certain gases in the Earth's atmosphere that trap heat from the sun, leading to warming of the planet. These gases are often referred to as greenhouse gases (GHGs). The main gases contributing to the greenhouse effect and commonly measured for their environmental impact include:\n\n1. **Carbon Dioxide (CO2)**: This is one of the most significant greenhouse gases and is produced through various human activities such as burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial processes.\n\n2. **Methane (CH4)**: Methane is produced from agricultural activities (such as livestock farming), waste management practices, and energy production. It has a much higher global warming potential than CO2 over shorter time periods.\n\n3. **Nitrous Oxide (N2O)**: This gas is released during agricultural and industrial activities, as well as from burning fossil fuels. Like methane, it has a stronger warming effect per molecule compared to carbon dioxide.\n\n4. **Water Vapor (H2O)**: While water vapor is not directly emitted by human activities in the same way as other GHGs, changes in temperature can increase the amount of water vapor in the atmosphere, which then contributes to further warming.\n\n5. **Fluorinated Gases**: These include hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), perfluorocarbons (PFCs), sulfur hexafluoride (SF6), and nitrogen trifluoride (NF3). They are used in various industrial processes and have very high global warming potentials.\n\nThese gases are monitored and regulated under international agreements like the Kyoto Protocol and the Paris Agreement to mitigate climate change."
    },
