      "problem": "Which certifications ensure the sustainability of cotton products mentioned in the context?",
      "solution": "Better Cotton Initiative, Fairtrade Cotton, Global Organic Textile Standard (GOTS), Organic Cotton Standard, Recycled Claim Standard (RCS), organic cotton products",
      "attempt": "The context you're referring to isn't provided here, but generally, there are several certifications that ensure the sustainability of cotton products. Here are some of the most recognized ones:\n\n1. **Organic Cotton Standard (OCS)**: This certification ensures that a product contains a certain amount of organic material and is processed according to specific social and environmental criteria.\n\n2. **Global Organic Textile Standard (GOTS)**: This is one of the leading textile processing standards for organic fibers. It includes both ecological and social criteria and bans all kinds of toxic chemicals and heavy metals.\n\n3. **Better Cotton Initiative (BCI)**: BCI works with farmers to help them improve their farming practices, reduce the use of water, energy, and harmful chemicals, and increase productivity and income.\n\n4. **Fair Trade Certified**: While not exclusively for cotton, Fair Trade certification ensures fair wages and good working conditions for workers involved in the production process.\n\n5. **Cotton Made in Africa (CmiA)**: This initiative focuses on sustainable cotton production in Sub-Saharan Africa, aiming to improve the living conditions of smallholder farmers while protecting the environment.\n\n6. **Textile Exchange's Recycled Claim Standard (RCS)**: Although this is more about recycled materials, it can apply to recycled cotton as well, ensuring that products contain a minimum of 5% recycled content.\n\nEach of these certifications has its own set of requirements and verification processes to ensure that the cotton used in products meets high standards of sustainability."
