      "question": "What activities are included in Lenzing's Scope 3 emissions reporting under its sustainability framework?",
      "answer": "Scope 3 emissions, chemicals & pulp, production of fuels, production of purchased materials, transportation of fibers to customers, transportation of purchased raw materials & fuels",
      "type": "Factoid",
      "tag": "Sustainability",
      "question_id": "train:000944:qa_pairs_factoid:0009",
      "difficulty_id": "F-000014",
      "id": "train:000944:qa_pairs_factoid:0009",
      "problem": "What activities are included in Lenzing's Scope 3 emissions reporting under its sustainability framework?",
      "solution": "Scope 3 emissions, chemicals & pulp, production of fuels, production of purchased materials, transportation of fibers to customers, transportation of purchased raw materials & fuels",
      "attempt": "Lenzing, a global leader in the production of fibers from sustainable sources, has a comprehensive approach to sustainability that includes detailed reporting on greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions across all scopes as defined by the Greenhouse Gas Protocol. Scope 3 emissions encompass all indirect emissions that occur in a company’s value chain and can be broadly categorized into upstream and downstream activities.\n\nFor Lenzing, Scope 3 emissions reporting would include a wide range of activities, which can be broken down into several categories:\n\n1. **Upstream Activities:**\n   - Purchased goods and services: This includes emissions associated with raw materials such as wood pulp, chemicals, and energy used in the production process.\n   - Capital goods: Emissions related to the manufacturing and disposal of machinery and equipment used in production.\n   - Fuel- and energy-related activities not otherwise captured: This could include emissions from electricity and heat purchased from external suppliers.\n   - Upstream transportation and distribution: Emissions from transporting raw materials and finished products to and from suppliers and distributors.\n   - Waste generated in operations: Emissions resulting from waste management practices, including the treatment and disposal of waste.\n   - Business travel: Emissions from employee travel for business purposes.\n   - Employee commuting: Emissions from employees traveling to and from work.\n   - Upstream leased assets: Emissions related to leased assets used in operations.\n\n2. **Downstream Activities:**\n   - Transportation and distribution: Emissions from delivering finished products to customers.\n   - Processing of sold products: Emissions from any further processing or transformation of Lenzing’s products by customers.\n   - Use of sold products: Emissions from the use phase of Lenzing’s products, which is particularly relevant for long-lasting products like textiles.\n   - End-of-life treatment of sold products: Emissions from the disposal or recycling of Lenzing’s products at the end of their life cycle.\n   - Leased assets: Emissions from assets leased out by Lenzing.\n   - Franchises: Emissions from franchises if applicable.\n   - Investments: Emissions related to investments made by Lenzing.\n\nLenzing aims to reduce its environmental impact through sustainable practices and innovation, and this includes efforts to minimize Scope 3 emissions. By addressing these various aspects of its value chain, Lenzing works towards achieving its sustainability goals and contributing to global climate change mitigation efforts."
    },
