      "attempt": "CA Immo, a leading real estate company in Central and Eastern Europe, adheres to various sustainability standards to ensure that its buildings meet high environmental and social criteria. While CA Immo does not specify a single international standard as the sole certification method for all its properties, it generally aligns with several widely recognized frameworks. These include:\n\n1. **BREEAM (Building Research Establishment Environmental Assessment Method)**: This is one of the world's leading sustainability assessment methods for structures. BREEAM assesses the environmental, economic, and social performance of buildings.\n\n2. **LEED (Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design)**: Developed by the U.S. Green Building Council (USGBC), LEED is a globally recognized benchmark for the design, construction, operation, and maintenance of green buildings.\n\n3. **DGNB (Deutsche Gesellschaft für Nachhaltiges Bauen)**: The German Sustainable Building Council’s DGNB System is an international certification system for sustainable building projects. It evaluates the ecological, economic, sociocultural, and technical functional aspects of buildings.\n\n4. **Passivhaus Standard**: This standard focuses on energy efficiency and aims to reduce the need for heating and cooling in buildings through passive design strategies.\n\n5. **Energy Performance Certificates (EPCs)**: While not a certification per se, EPCs are mandatory in many countries and provide a standardized method of rating a building's energy efficiency.\n\nCA Immo often targets these certifications to improve the sustainability profile of its properties, aiming to create healthier, more efficient, and environmentally friendly spaces. However, the specific standards applied can vary based on the location of the property and local regulations or preferences. For the most accurate and up-to-date information, it would be best to consult CA Immo's official communications or reports."
    },
