      "problem": "What strategies does 1&1 implement to reduce its environmental impact?",
      "solution": "decarbonisation, energy consumption, energy management, energy-saving systems, indirect emissions, renewable energies",
      "attempt": "1&1, now known as 1&1 IONOS, is a web hosting and online services provider that has implemented several strategies to reduce its environmental impact. Here are some of the key initiatives:\n\n1. **Renewable Energy Sources**: 1&1 IONOS aims to power its data centers with renewable energy sources. This includes using wind and solar power to reduce reliance on fossil fuels.\n\n2. **Energy-Efficient Data Centers**: The company focuses on building and operating energy-efficient data centers. This involves using advanced cooling systems, efficient hardware, and optimizing server usage to minimize energy consumption.\n\n3. **Carbon Footprint Reduction**: 1&1 IONOS works towards reducing its carbon footprint through various measures, including improving energy efficiency, investing in renewable energy projects, and offsetting unavoidable emissions.\n\n4. **Sustainable Hosting Solutions**: By offering green hosting options, customers can choose hosting plans that are powered by renewable energy, helping them reduce their own environmental impact.\n\n5. **Eco-Friendly Practices**: The company also promotes eco-friendly practices within its operations, such as reducing paper usage, encouraging digital communication, and implementing recycling programs.\n\n6. **Partnerships and Certifications**: 1&1 IONOS collaborates with organizations focused on sustainability and may seek certifications that validate their commitment to environmental protection.\n\n7. **Customer Education**: Educating customers about the importance of sustainable practices and how they can contribute to reducing their digital footprint is another strategy employed by 1&1 IONOS.\n\nBy focusing on these areas, 1&1 IONOS aims to not only reduce its own environmental impact but also encourage its customers and partners to do the same, contributing to a more sustainable internet ecosystem."
